The {{Nihongo|'''Yuzawa Incident'''|湯沢の変|Yuzawa no Hen}} was a raid and abduction event that occurred on the night of October 12, 1558, when forces of the [[Ogasawara clan]] under {{nihongo|[[Ogasawara Terumune]]|小笠原輝宗}} breached the [[Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace]] in [[Mino Province]] and seized the two-year-old Imperial Prince [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Fumihito]] — son of Crown Prince Yoshihito (later [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]) — from the palace grounds. During the raid, Prince Mutsuhito, the Crown Prince's brother, was killed attempting to intercept the attackers. The Ogasawara subsequently used the hostage prince to extort massive territorial concessions from the Imperial Court, then falsely reported the child's death and abandoned him at [[Shōhei Temple]], a remote monastery in the Mino mountains.

The prince survived. Raised as a commoner acolyte named {{nihongo|"Ichimatsu"|一松|extra="First Pine"}}, he was eventually adopted by {{nihongo|[[Honda Shigetatsu]]|本多重辰}} of the [[Honda clan]] and given the name {{nihongo|[[Honda Shigetada]]|本多重忠}} — the man who would ultimately destroy the Ogasawara clan at the [[Battle of Tone]] and be revealed, in January 1601, as the lost Prince Fumihito, the rightful heir to the throne.

The ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' — the official biographical record of the Honda family, compiled in 1680 — frames the Yuzawa Incident as the foundational crime of the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]]: the original sin from which every subsequent act of violence, betrayal, and retribution flowed. The Ogasawara did not merely steal a child. They stole the instrument of their own destruction, raised it in ignorance, and placed it in the hands of their enemies — and they did not know it for forty-three years.

==Background==

===The erosion of Imperial authority===

By the mid-16th century, the authority of the Imperial Family in [[Okaiken]] (then known as [[Mitoyagi]]) had eroded to the point of irrelevance. The [[Minato Shogunate|Minato clan]], which nominally governed the archipelago from the capital at [[Okami]], was a hollow institution incapable of enforcing its will beyond the capital's immediate vicinity. The [[Jofun Code]]'s administrative framework — the system of provincial governors, estate (''shōen'') holders, and court-appointed magistrates that had structured Mitoyagi's governance for centuries — had collapsed into a patchwork of local warlords who ruled by force and recognised the Emperor only as a spiritual figurehead.

The Imperial Court retained its ceremonial functions: it signed the land grants that legitimised a ''daimyō''{{'}}s hold over his territory, it appointed the court titles that determined a lord's formal rank, and it performed the rituals that, in the Sanese worldview, sustained the cosmic order. But the court possessed no military force of its own. Its physical security depended on the willingness of regional lords to respect the deepest taboo in Sanese political culture: the inviolability of the Imperial person. For over a century, that taboo had held. No warlord, however ambitious, had dared to lay hands on a member of the Imperial Family.

The Yuzawa Incident would shatter that taboo forever.

===The Ogasawara clan===

{{Main|Ogasawara clan}}

The [[Ogasawara clan]], based in [[Mino Province]] in the mountainous interior of central [[Yagikoshu]], was one of the most aggressive rising powers of the era. Under the leadership of Ogasawara Terumune, who had inherited the clan leadership around 1530, the Ogasawara had spent three decades expanding from a minor mountain house into the dominant power in the Mino valley. Terumune's methods — marriage alliances, targeted assassinations through networks of paid ''ronin'' and mercenaries, and the systematic absorption of neighbouring minor houses — had transformed a domain of perhaps 200,000 ''koku'' into a regional power with ambitions far beyond the Mino valley.

By the early 1550s, Terumune had concluded that the Ogasawara's continued rise required more than territorial expansion. It required ''legitimacy'' — the kind of legitimacy that only the Imperial Court could confer. Terumune sought two things from the court: official court titles that would elevate the Ogasawara above their rivals in the formal hierarchy, and the appointment of an Ogasawara heir as ''Sei-i Taishōgun'' — the military title that would transform a provincial warlord into the nominal ruler of the archipelago.

The Imperial Court, impoverished and powerless, had neither the will nor the ability to grant such concessions to a provincial upstart. The court's refusal was not a matter of principle but of precedent: if the Ogasawara could buy the Shogunate, then every warlord in Mitoyagi would descend on the capital to outbid them. The court's only protection was its irrelevance — the assumption that no one cared enough about its decrees to fight for them.

Terumune decided to prove that assumption wrong.

===The Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace===

The [[Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace]] was a seasonal retreat of the Imperial family, located in the Yuzawa valley approximately twelve kilometres south of the Ogasawara's territory at [[Kasagi]]. The palace was a modest complex of wooden buildings surrounded by gardens and hot springs — a place of quiet retreat, not a military installation. Its security relied on two factors: the small force of Imperial retainers who accompanied the court on its seasonal progresses, and the [[Miyamori clan]], the local guardian house that maintained the Kasagi-Yuzawa corridor.

The Miyamori were the last obstacle between the Ogasawara and the palace. In the autumn of 1557 — a year before the raid — Terumune's son and military commander, [[Ogasawara Terunobu]], led a force of approximately 1,500 troops against [[Kasagi Castle]], the Miyamori stronghold. The castle fell within two days. The Miyamori head, [[Miyamori Tadatsune]], was captured, tortured for three days for intelligence about the palace's security arrangements, and burned alive when he refused to speak. His younger brother, [[Miyamori Tadakane]], escaped through the mountains to [[Nagayama]], where he would eventually find service with the [[Honda clan]] — a chain of events that, four decades later, would connect the destruction of the Miyamori to the rise of the Honda Shogunate.

With the Miyamori destroyed and the Kasagi-Yuzawa corridor under Ogasawara control, the Mikasawa Palace was isolated. The approach routes from the south and east were in Ogasawara hands. The palace's garrison — perhaps two hundred palace guards — was the only force standing between the Ogasawara and the Imperial family.

===The Imperial progress of 1558===

In the autumn of 1558, the Imperial family traveled to the Mikasawa Palace for its seasonal retreat. The party included Crown Prince Yoshihito (later Emperor Komei), his wife Empress Sento-in, his brother Prince Mutsuhito, and his infant son — the two-year-old Prince Fumihito. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the progress was routine, following a pattern that the court had observed for decades without incident. No one in the Imperial party had reason to believe that the autumn of 1558 would be different from any other.

The Ogasawara had been planning the operation for at least a year. The destruction of the Miyamori had given them intelligence about the palace's layout, its garrison strength, and the seasonal patterns of the Imperial household. Terumune's strategy was not assassination but ''leverage'': the abduction of a royal child would give the Ogasawara a bargaining chip that the court could not ignore. A dead prince was a martyr; a living prince was a hostage. And a hostage could be traded for titles, territory, and the formal recognition that the Ogasawara craved.

The ''Mitsukoji'' describes the planning with a cold precision that makes the operation more, not less, chilling:

{{quote|The lord of Mino counted the guards, counted the doors, counted the candles, and counted the child's years. Two years old. Old enough to survive a journey. Young enough to be carried in the dark without a cry. The arithmetic was perfect. The arithmetic was always perfect.|''Mitsukoji'', Book I}}

==The raid==

===The night of October 12===

On the night of October 12, 1558, a force of Ogasawara soldiers and agents under the command of Ogasawara Terunobu moved south from Kasagi toward the Mikasawa Palace. The ''Mitsukoji'' estimates the force at approximately 1,500 men — a mixture of Ogasawara retainers, mercenary ''ronin'', and paid agents who had been infiltrated into the palace's service staff over the preceding months. The operation was timed for the hours after midnight, when the palace's guards would be at their thinnest and the household would be asleep.

The approach was facilitated by the Ogasawara's control of the Kasagi-Yuzawa corridor. The force moved through the mountain passes under cover of darkness, avoiding the main roads and arriving at the palace's northern perimeter shortly before the Hour of the Ox (approximately 2:00 AM). The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the night was overcast — the moon hidden behind cloud cover — and that the autumn rains had softened the ground, muffling the sound of footsteps on the palace paths.

===The breach===

The Ogasawara's agents inside the palace — men who had been installed as servants, gardeners, and kitchen workers over the preceding months — opened the northern service gate shortly after the assault force arrived. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the gate was opened by a man named {{nihongo|Gorōza|五郎座}}, a kitchen servant who had been in Ogasawara pay for less than a year. The gate's two guards were killed silently — their throats cut before they could raise an alarm.

With the northern gate open, the main assault force entered the palace grounds in three columns. The first column moved toward the guards' quarters, with orders to neutralise the palace garrison before they could organise a defence. The second column moved toward the residential wing, where the Imperial family was housed. The third column — a smaller force of hand-picked men — moved directly toward the quarters of the young Prince Fumihito.

The assault on the guards' quarters was brief but violent. The palace guards, most of them asleep, were overwhelmed before they could arm themselves. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the guards' quarters were set ablaze to create confusion and to prevent any surviving guards from regrouping. The firelight illuminated the palace grounds, casting the assault force in sharp silhouette against the burning buildings.

===The death of Prince Mutsuhito===

In the residential wing, the noise of the assault on the guards' quarters woke the Imperial household. Crown Prince Yoshihito, Empress Sento-in, and Prince Mutsuhito were housed in separate chambers within the same wing. The young Prince Fumihito slept in a nursery attended by two nursemaids.

Prince Mutsuhito — the Crown Prince's younger brother, a man in his early twenties — was the first member of the Imperial family to encounter the attackers. According to the ''Mitsukoji'', Mutsuhito heard the sounds of the assault on the guards' quarters, armed himself with a ''wakizashi'' (short sword), and moved toward the nursery to protect his nephew. He encountered a group of Ogasawara soldiers in the corridor connecting the residential chambers to the nursery.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Mutsuhito fought the attackers in the narrow corridor, killing two soldiers before being overwhelmed. The exact circumstances of his death are disputed: the ''Mitsukoji'' states that he was killed by multiple sword wounds, "not in the manner of a warrior's death, but in the manner of a man who stands in a doorway and will not move." The ''Mitsukoji''{{'}}s account emphasises that Mutsuhito's stand, though futile, delayed the attackers long enough for one of the nursemaids to attempt to flee with the child.

===The abduction===

The nursemaid — identified in the ''Mitsukoji'' only as {{nihongo|"the woman from the northern country"|北国の女|Kitakuni no Onna}} — seized the two-year-old prince and attempted to escape through the nursery's rear entrance, which led to the palace's inner garden. She was intercepted by the third column — the hand-picked force that had been assigned specifically to secure the prince.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the nursemaid was killed attempting to shield the child. The prince, two years old, was taken from her body. The ''Mitsukoji'' does not describe the child's condition in detail, noting only that "the prince was alive, and silent, and cold." The silence of the child — a two-year-old who did not cry — is noted by the ''Mitsukoji'' as a detail that the chroniclers found difficult to explain. The text offers no interpretation, only the observation.

Prince Fumihito was wrapped in a cloth and carried out through the northern gate. The Ogasawara force withdrew from the palace grounds in good order, leaving the guards' quarters burning and the residential wing in chaos. The entire operation, from the opening of the northern gate to the withdrawal of the assault force, lasted approximately one hour.

Crown Prince Yoshihito survived the raid uninjured. He had been in his chambers when the assault began and had been unable to reach his brother or his son in time. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that when the Crown Prince reached the nursery and found the body of the nursemaid and the absence of his son, he stood in the doorway for a long time and did not speak.

==Aftermath==

===The Ogasawara demands===

Within days of the raid, the Ogasawara clan communicated its demands to the Imperial Court through intermediary channels. The demands were unprecedented in their scope:

* '''Territorial concession''': The legal lordship over the northern quarter of the island of [[Harujima]], a strategic territory that would give the Ogasawara control over key trade routes and a foothold on the second-largest island of the archipelago.
* '''Economic concession''': The transfer of ''shōen'' (estate) rights amounting to 300,000 ''koku'' to Ogasawara retainers, bringing the clan's total assessed wealth to approximately 1.5 million ''koku'' — a staggering figure that would transform the Ogasawara from a regional power into a national one.
* '''Political concession''': The appointment of Ogasawara Terunobu as ''Bugyō'' (Magistrate) of the Imperial Court, giving a warlord direct administrative authority over Imperial affairs for the first time in centuries.

The demands were accompanied by an explicit threat: if the court did not comply, the prince would be killed. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Ogasawara Terunobu — the son who had led the raid — sent a personal message to Crown Prince Yoshihito: "The child's life is measured in the court's reply. Each day of delay is a day taken from his years."

===The Imperial Court's response===

Crown Prince Yoshihito initially refused to capitulate. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the Crown Prince, despite his grief and rage, upheld the dignity of the throne, arguing that submission to a warlord's extortion would destroy the court's remaining authority more thoroughly than the loss of any single prince. The court's senior advisors, however, were less resolute. Several of them had been in Ogasawara pay for years, and they argued — with varying degrees of sincerity — that the prince's life was worth any concession.

The negotiations reached a breaking point when Ogasawara Terunobu sent a second message: a lock of the child's hair, wrapped in white silk, accompanied by a note that read: "The next package will be heavier." The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Empress Sento-in — the child's mother — collapsed upon receiving the package, and that Crown Prince Yoshihito, upon seeing his wife's grief, finally agreed to the Ogasawara terms.

The concessions were formally granted in a court ceremony in November 1558. The Ogasawara received the northern quarter of Harujima, the 300,000 ''koku'' of ''shōen'' rights, and the ''Bugyō'' appointment. The court's scribes recorded the concessions as voluntary grants — a fiction that preserved the court's dignity while surrendering its substance. The ''Mitsukoji'' notes the irony: "The court gave the Ogasawara everything they asked for, and called it generosity. The Ogasawara took everything the court gave, and called it right."

===The betrayal===

Despite the Imperial Court fulfilling all demands, the Ogasawara clan did not return Prince Fumihito. The reasons for this decision are not fully clear from the surviving sources. The ''Mitsukoji'' suggests several factors:

First, the Ogasawara's sudden rise — from a provincial house to a national power in the space of a single negotiation — had alarmed rival clans across Mitoyagi. Several houses had begun to mobilise their forces in response to the Ogasawara's acquisition of Harujima, and the Ogasawara leadership feared that the return of the prince would be followed by a coordinated attack on their newly expanded territory. A living prince in the Ogasawara's hands was a deterrent; a returned prince was a debt paid.

Second, the Ogasawara feared that the court would renege on the concessions once the prince was returned. The ''Bugyō'' appointment, in particular, was a prize that the court might revoke once the immediate crisis passed. Keeping the prince as a hostage ensured the court's continued compliance.

Third — and most critically — the Ogasawara leadership recognised that a living Imperial prince in their custody was a permanent liability. If the prince died in their care, the court would treat it as murder. If the prince escaped, the court would rally the other clans against the Ogasawara. If the prince was returned, the Ogasawara would lose their leverage. In each scenario, the prince's continued existence was a problem.

The Ogasawara chose a fourth option. They reported to the court that the young prince had contracted a sudden illness and died during the journey back to the capital. The report was accompanied by a small coffin containing the body of a child of approximately the right age — a body that the ''Mitsukoji'' identifies, without elaboration, as "not the prince's."

The court accepted the report. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Crown Prince Yoshihito, upon receiving the news, "did not weep, because he did not believe it, and he did not disbelieve it, because he could not afford to." The ambiguity — the possibility that his son might still be alive — would haunt the Crown Prince for the next forty-three years.

==The abandoned prince==

===The abandonment at Shōhei Temple===

Instead of returning the prince or killing him, the Ogasawara abandoned the child at [[Shōhei Temple]], a remote [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] monastery in the mountains of Mino Province. The temple was a modest institution — a community of perhaps twenty monks, led by the elderly abbot {{nihongo|Zen no Kishō|善の喜勝}}, who maintained the temple's gardens and performed the daily rituals of the [[Jōdo-shū]] sect. The temple's remoteness made it an ideal place to dispose of a child without attracting attention: it was too small to matter, too poor to be searched, and too isolated for the child to be found by anyone who was not looking for him.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the child was left at the temple gate in the early hours of a winter morning, wrapped in a rough cloth, with no identifying marks and no note. The Ogasawara agents who delivered the child were under orders to leave no trace of the prince's identity. The child was simply — abandoned.

Zen no Kishō took the child in. The abbot, who had no way of knowing the boy's identity, named him {{nihongo|"Ichimatsu"|一松|extra="First Pine"}} — a commoner's name, a name that carried no weight, no history, no implication of rank or lineage. The name was chosen, the ''Mitsukoji'' suggests, because the boy was found beside a pine tree in the temple garden, and because the abbot believed that a name should describe what is, not what might have been.

===The monastic years===

Ichimatsu was raised as a commoner acolyte. The ''Mitsukoji'' and the ''Honda Letters'' provide fragmentary details of his childhood at Shōhei Temple:

The boy was educated in classical texts and Baiduist doctrine, as was customary for temple acolytes. He was trained in the temple's daily labors — sweeping the garden, tending the vegetable plots, carrying water from the mountain spring, and assisting the older monks in the preparation of ritual materials. The life was austere, disciplined, and far removed from the sheltered world of the Imperial court.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Ichimatsu was, by the abbot's account, a quiet child — "too quiet for a boy of his years, as though he had learned at an age too young that silence was safer than speech." The chroniclers do not speculate on whether this temperament was innate or whether it was the residue of the trauma of the raid — the two-year-old child who had been taken from his nursemaid's body and who, the ''Mitsukoji'' had noted, "was alive, and silent, and cold." The silence of the child at the palace and the silence of the boy at the temple are presented, without comment, as the same silence.

The boy was converted to the Jōdo-shū sect of Baiduism and was raised with no knowledge of his Imperial lineage. Zen no Kishō, who had no reason to suspect the child's identity, treated him as an ordinary foundling — one of the many children abandoned at temples during the chaos of the Sengoku period. The abbot's kindness was not unusual; it was the ordinary compassion of a monk who had taken in a child who needed a home.

The ''Mitsukoji'' reflects on this period with a quality that is rare in the chronicle — a tenderness that stands in contrast to the catalogue of violence that fills the surrounding pages:

{{quote|The prince who should have been raised in silk was raised in rough cloth. The prince who should have been carried was made to carry water. The prince who should have been served was taught to serve. And the prince who should have been known by his name was given a name that no one remembered. The mountain kept him, and the temple fed him, and the pine tree gave him shade, and none of them knew what they were keeping.|''Mitsukoji'', Book I}}

===The adoption by Honda Shigetatsu===

{{Main|Honda Shigetatsu}}

Sometime between 1560 and 1562 — most scholars place the event in the autumn of 1560 — Ichimatsu's life changed again. {{nihongo|Honda Shigetatsu|本多重辰}}, the leader of the [[Honda clan]] of [[Kazusa Province]] in [[Harujima]], was visiting Shōhei Temple on a pilgrimage during a lull in the Ogasawara campaigns. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigetatsu observed the young boy — a child of approximately four — during a temple festival, and was struck by the child's extraordinary composure and physical coordination.

According to the chronicle, Shigetatsu asked Zen no Kishō about the boy's parentage and was told only that he was "a foundling of unclear origin." Modern historians have debated whether Shigetatsu suspected the boy's true identity. The more likely explanation, supported by the ''Mitsukoji''{{'}}s account, is that Shigetatsu recognized exceptional potential in the child — consistent with the ''[[Gekokujō]]'' ethos of the Sengoku period, which valued ability over birth. Shigetatsu, who had built the Honda clan on the principle of promoting by competence rather than lineage, saw in the boy a quality that transcended his obscure origins.

Shigetatsu formally adopted Ichimatsu into the Honda clan, giving him the name {{nihongo|Honda Shigetada|本多重忠}} and raising him as a warrior alongside his biological sons. The adoption was consistent with Sengoku-era social mobility: a warlord could adopt a commoner, a monk, or a foundling based on perceived potential, regardless of bloodline. The supreme irony — that the boy raised as a commoner was actually the Imperial heir, the son of the Crown Prince whose son had been stolen by the very clan the Honda now served — would not be revealed for another four decades.

The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the adoption as the moment at which the Ogasawara's crime began to turn against them:

{{quote|The Ogasawara had stolen a prince and abandoned him. The mountain had kept him. The temple had fed him. And now the Honda — the clan that served the Ogasawara, that fought the Ogasawara's wars, that bled for the Ogasawara's ambition — took the abandoned child and raised him as their own. The Ogasawara had given their enemy the weapon that would destroy them, and they had done it with their own hands. They had stolen the child from the Emperor and given him to the Honda. They had not known what they were doing. They never knew.|''Mitsukoji'', Book I}}

===The transformation===

Under Shigetatsu's guidance, the temple acolyte became a warrior. Shigetada — as the boy was now known — was raised with the same discipline and meritocratic expectations that governed the Honda clan's military culture. By his early teens, he was already demonstrating the extraordinary combat abilities that would later earn him the epithet "The Warrior who Surpassed Death." He was trained in the use of the arquebus, the spear, and the sword; he was educated in military strategy, logistics, and governance; and he was instilled with the Honda clan's core philosophy — that power should be distributed by function, not concentrated by birthright.

The boy who had been raised to carry water now carried a sword. The boy who had been taught to serve now learned to command. The boy who had been named for a pine tree now bore the name of a warrior house. And the boy who had been stolen from an emperor now served the clan that would destroy the men who stole him.

The transformation was complete. Prince Fumihito — the child of the Imperial court, the son of an emperor, the heir to the throne — was gone. In his place stood Honda Shigetada, a warrior of the Honda clan, a general in the service of Ogasawara Terumasa, a man with no name, no lineage, and no memory of the palace from which he had been taken.

He did not know who he was. No one did. And for forty-three years, that ignorance — the ignorance of the stolen child, the ignorance of the clan that raised him, the ignorance of the clan that stole him — would be the most powerful force in the archipelago.

==The secret==

===How the identity remained hidden===

The secret of Honda Shigetada's identity survived for over four decades through a combination of obscurity, distance, and the Ogasawara's own deception. The Ogasawara had reported the prince's death in 1558, and the court had accepted the report. No one was looking for a living prince because no one believed the prince was alive. The child had been abandoned at a remote temple with no identifying marks, raised under a commoner's name, and adopted by a minor clan hundreds of kilometres from the capital. The chain of knowledge — from the Ogasawara agents who had delivered the child to Shōhei Temple, to the abbot who had taken him in, to the Honda lord who had adopted him — was so thin and so dispersed that the truth could have been lost forever.

The Ogasawara themselves did not know where the prince was. The agents who had abandoned the child at Shōhei Temple had been ordered to report the temple's name to Terumune, but the report was lost in the chaos of the Ogasawara's subsequent campaigns. By the 1580s, when Ogasawara Terumasa — the Demon King — had consolidated his power, the knowledge of the prince's survival had been buried so deep that even the clan's own records no longer contained it. The Ogasawara had committed the perfect crime and then forgotten the evidence.

Zen no Kishō, the abbot who had raised the boy, did not know the child's identity. Honda Shigetatsu, who had adopted the boy, did not know. Honda Shigetada himself did not know. The only people who knew that the prince was alive were the Ogasawara agents who had abandoned him — and they did not know where he was.

===Kazumasu's discovery===

{{Main|Takigawa Kazumasu}}

The secret was rediscovered sometime between 1587 and 1590 by [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] — the peasant-born general who had risen through the Ogasawara ranks on merit and who had become Ogasawara Terumasa's most trusted retainer. Kazumasu, who would later betray and kill Terumasa at the [[Honto-ji Incident]] of 1592, was the architect of the Ogasawara's intelligence network — a system of agents, informants, and intercepted communications that gave the Ogasawara unparalleled knowledge of their rivals' intentions.

The exact mechanism of Kazumasu's discovery is not recorded in the ''Mitsukoji'' with certainty. The chronicle suggests that Kazumasu's agents, in the course of cataloguing the Ogasawara clan's historical records during the consolidation of the 1580s, uncovered documents relating to the Yuzawa Incident — including the report on the prince's "death" and references to the temple where the child had been abandoned. Kazumasu, who was known for his obsessive attention to detail and his ability to see patterns that others missed, recognised the discrepancy: the report of the prince's death was too convenient, the temple too remote, the timing too precise. He dispatched agents to investigate.

The agents traced the child to Shōhei Temple, then to the Honda clan, and finally to Honda Shigetada — who was by this time a general in Ogasawara Terumasa's service, a man in his early thirties who had already distinguished himself in the campaigns of the early 1580s. The agents confirmed the match: the ages, the dates, the physical descriptions, and the temple records all pointed to the same conclusion. Honda Shigetada was Prince Fumihito.

Kazumasu did not report this discovery to Terumasa. He kept it for himself — a weapon more powerful than any army, a secret that could paralyze a general, destabilize a clan, and reshape the political landscape of the archipelago.

===The weapon===

Kazumasu used the secret in two directions.

Against Terumasa, Kazumasu used the secret to undermine the Ogasawara's confidence in their most capable general. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Kazumasu, in a private conversation with Terumasa, remarked: "The Honda general has a blood claim that no battlefield can settle." The implication — that Shigetada's Imperial bloodline gave him a legitimacy that no military victory could confer — planted a seed of suspicion in Terumasa's paranoid mind. The Demon King, who had built his power on the principle that merit mattered more than birth, was now confronted with the possibility that his most capable general was, by blood, the rightful heir to the throne. The irony was lost on Terumasa. It was not lost on Kazumasu.

Against Shigetada, Kazumasu used the secret as a leash. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Kazumasu sent a private message to Shigetada — the exact wording of which is not preserved, but the substance of which is recorded as: "The prince's health is a matter of state." The message was a threat: if Shigetada acted against Kazumasu or the Takigawa faction, Kazumasu would reveal the prince's identity — and the revelation would destroy everything Shigetada had built. A prince who was known to be a prince could no longer be a general. A prince who was known to be a prince would become a pawn of every faction that sought to use his bloodline. The secret that had been Shigetada's hidden strength — the legitimacy that no one knew he possessed — would become, if revealed, his prison.

The leash worked. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigetada, upon receiving Kazumasu's message, "could not act against the man who held his name." The general who had been forged in the fires of the Sengoku period — the warrior who had surpassed death, the commander who had never lost a battle — was paralyzed. Not by force, not by threat of violence, but by three words whispered in a message: ''the prince's health is a matter of state.''

===The years of paralysis===

The leash held for over a decade. Between 1589 and 1600, Shigetada — the most capable military commander in the archipelago — was unable to act against the Takigawa faction, even as they engineered the deaths of the people he loved.

In 1589, the Takigawa faction engineered the death of [[Anjō Motonobu]] — Shigetada's father-in-law, the naval commander who had built the invasion fleet at Tsuda, the man whose daughter Hatsu-no-kata had married Shigetada and whose grandchild would become the future Emperor Daigo. Motonobu was killed at the [[Battle of Saikawa]] in October 1589, when the Takigawa camp withheld reinforcements and "permitted" the interception of Shigetada's messenger. The ''Mitsukoji'' records: "The pen-pusher did not hold the blade at Saikawa. He did not need to. He needed only to hold the letter, and to let it go at the right moment." Shigetada, paralyzed by the leash, could not intervene.

In 1592, the Takigawa faction engineered the death of Ogasawara Terumasa himself at the [[Honto-ji Incident]]. Kazumasu, who had spent years positioning his forces and manipulating Terumasa's movements, surrounded the temple at [[Honto-ji]] with 1,000 men and set it ablaze. Terumasa's last words, recorded by the ''Mitsukoji'', were: "I raised a serpent in my bosom." Honda forces were three days' march away — exactly where Kazumasu's manipulation had placed them. Shigetada, paralyzed by the leash, could not reach his lord in time.

Between 1592 and 1600, during the period known as the [[Takigawa Terror]], Shigetada was kept in what the ''Mitsukoji'' calls "armed patience" — a state of readiness without action, a warrior who could not fight, a general who could not move. The leash held. The weapon held. The secret held.

For eight years, the most dangerous man in the archipelago was the most powerless — because the man who held his name could destroy him with a word.

==The miscalculation==

===The whisper that undid the whisperer===

The weapon that had held Shigetada in paralysis for over a decade was destroyed by the same man who had forged it — and it was destroyed by accident.

Sometime around 1598 — the exact date is not recorded — Takigawa Kazumasu, in a private discussion with [[Yamagata Masakage]], made a remark that would alter the course of the Sengoku period. Yamagata was a member of the [[Council of Four Elders]] — the governing body that Kazumasu had established to rule the archipelago after Terumasa's death — and was, as far as Kazumasu knew, a loyal Takigawa supporter. The discussion, according to the ''Mitsukoji'', concerned the disposition of the Honda clan and the question of whether Shigetada could be trusted to remain compliant.

In the course of the discussion, Kazumasu referred to Shigetada by a title that he had never used aloud before. He said, in a phrase that the ''Mitsukoji'' preserves with the precision of a dagger: {{nihongo|"The Prince, Shigetada"|王子、重忠|Ōji, Shigetada}}.

Three words. A title and a name, joined in a single breath. The master of whispers — the man who had controlled the fate of the archipelago through the careful management of secrets — had whispered his own.

===Yamagata Masakage===

{{Main|Yamagata Masakage}}

Yamagata Masakage had been placed on the Council of Four Elders by Kazumasu, who valued Yamagata's military competence and believed him to be a reliable, if not especially ambitious, ally. Yamagata had served the Takigawa faithfully — he had won the [[Battle of Shizugatake]] for Kazumasu in August 1592, defeating the Ogasawara loyalist forces under Ogasawara Hidemitsu. But the ''Mitsukoji'' records that Yamagata's service at Shizugatake was performed "out of duty, not loyalty" — Yamagata had no personal allegiance to Kazumasu, and his presence on the Council was a matter of pragmatism, not conviction.

The three words — "The Prince, Shigetada" — changed everything. Yamagata, who had been a Takigawa supporter by circumstance, was now in possession of a secret that gave him a reason to switch sides. If Honda Shigetada was the lost Prince Fumihito — the son of Emperor Komei, the rightful heir to the throne — then the Honda clan was not merely a rival faction. It was the Imperial cause. And the Takigawa, who had paralyzed the prince with a threat and ruled the archipelago in his name, were not merely a rival faction. They were the enemies of the throne.

The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the miscalculation as the turning point of the entire Sengoku period — the moment at which the balance of power shifted from the Takigawa to the Honda:

{{quote|Kazumasu, who had whispered in every ear in the archipelago, who had planted secrets in every court and every camp, who had built an empire of whispers — Kazumasu whispered in the wrong ear. He whispered his own secret to a man who did not need it, and who did not want it, and who could not unhear it. The master of whispers was undone by his own whisper. The serpent who whispered in every ear did not hear the whisper in his own.|''Mitsukoji'', Book III}}

Yamagata did not immediately act on the knowledge. He was a patient man, and he understood that the secret was a weapon that could only be fired once. He spent the next two years quietly shifting his allegiance — building contacts within the Honda and the [[Ii clan|Ii]], positioning his forces for the moment when the leash would break, and waiting for the day when Kazumasu's death would free the prince to act.

The miscalculation — three words, spoken in a private room to a man who was supposed to be an ally — was the most consequential accident in the history of the Sengoku period. It turned the Council of Four Elders from a Takigawa instrument into a fractured body with a Honda/Ii sympathizer at its heart. It turned the Battle of Tone from a Honda defeat into a Honda victory. And it turned the stolen prince from a paralyzed general into an emperor.

Kazumasu never knew what he had done. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Yamagata gave no sign of his changed allegiance — he continued to serve on the Council, continued to attend Kazumasu's meetings, continued to perform his duties with the same competence that had earned him his position. The serpent who whispered in every ear did not hear the whisper in his own — because the whisper was silent, and the hearing was patient, and the patience was the one weapon that Kazumasu, who had built his empire on impatience and manipulation, could not counter.

==The anthology of revenge==

The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the Yuzawa Incident not as an isolated event but as the first page of an anthology of revenge that spans the entire Sengoku period. The chronicle's framing — which has shaped the Sanese historical understanding of the period for over three centuries — presents the incident as the crime, and everything that followed as the punishment. The Ogasawara raised the man who would tear down everything they built, and they did not know it.

The throughline, as traced by the ''Mitsukoji'', runs as follows:

===1558: The original sin===

The Ogasawara steal the prince. A child is taken from an emperor's palace in the night, his uncle is killed, his nursemaid is slain, and the boy is carried into the mountains. The Ogasawara extort their concessions, report the child's death, and abandon him at a temple. The crime is complete. The instrument of their destruction is in the world, and they do not know it.

===The prince grows as a nobody (1558–1587)===

The stolen prince is raised as a commoner — a temple acolyte named Ichimatsu, a boy who carries water and tends gardens and learns the silence of a child who has been taught that silence is safer than speech. He is adopted by the Honda clan, given a warrior's name, and forged into a general. He serves the Ogasawara — the clan that stole him — as a soldier. He fights their wars. He wins their battles. He is their instrument, and they do not know whose instrument he is.

===1587: The weapon is forged===

Kazumasu discovers the prince's identity and turns it into a weapon. The Ogasawara's own crime — the abduction that was meant to give them power over the Imperial Court — becomes a tool of manipulation in the hands of their most trusted retainer. The crime that was committed against the Emperor is now committed against the Emperor's son — not by force, but by secrecy. The prince is paralyzed by the knowledge of who he is. The Ogasawara's own general, fighting their wars, is their Emperor's heir — and the man who knows this is the man who will destroy them.

===1589: The father-in-law===

The weapon paralyzes the prince while the Takigawa kill his father-in-law. Anjō Motonobu — the naval commander, the father of Shigetada's wife, the grandfather of the future Emperor Daigo — is killed at the Battle of Saikawa because the Takigawa camp withheld reinforcements and permitted the interception of Shigetada's messenger. The prince, who could have saved him, is paralyzed by the leash. The ''Mitsukoji'': "The pen-pusher did not hold the blade at Saikawa. He did not need to. He needed only to hold the letter, and to let it go at the right moment."

===1592: The lord===

The weapon paralyzes the prince while the Takigawa kill his lord. Ogasawara Terumasa — the Demon King, the man who had commanded the prince's loyalty, the man whose wars the prince had fought — is betrayed and killed at the Honto-ji Incident. Terumasa's last words: "I raised a serpent in my bosom." The prince, who could have saved him, is three days' march away — exactly where Kazumasu's manipulation placed him. The leash holds. The weapon holds. The secret holds.

===1592–1600: Armed patience===

The weapon keeps the prince in "armed patience" for eight years. The Takigawa Terror — the period of Kazumasu's rule through the Council of Four Elders — holds the archipelago in its grip. The prince, the most capable military commander in the land, is unable to move. The stolen child, now a man in his forties, waits. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigetada, during these years, "polished his sword every morning and sheathed it every night, and the sword grew brighter and the sheath grew thinner, and neither was used."

===c. 1598: The miscalculation===

Kazumasu, the master of whispers, whispers his own secret. "The Prince, Shigetada." Three words, spoken in a private room to Yamagata Masakage, turn the Council of Four Elders from a Takigawa instrument into a fractured body. The serpent who whispered in every ear did not hear the whisper in his own. The weapon that had held the prince in paralysis for a decade is now in the hands of the prince's allies — and Kazumasu does not know it.

===June 1600: The weapon dies===

Kazumasu marches north against Honda-aligned [[Katsuura Castle]] and is shot by a sniper on June 18, 1600. He dies within minutes. The ''Mitsukoji'' records the death with a brevity that borders on contempt: "The serpent was struck in the field, and the field was no longer his." The weapon dies with him. The leash breaks. The secret that was a prison becomes a key.

===October 1600: The crack becomes a fracture===

The [[Battle of Tone]] — approximately 160,000 men, the largest battle in the history of the archipelago. The Eastern Army (Honda, Ii, with Yamagata Masakage's support) faces the Western Army (Takigawa, commanded by [[Mashita Morihiro]]). Yamagata Masakage, who had been a Takigawa supporter for eight years, brings his swords to the Honda. The crack that Kazumasu's miscalculation had opened becomes a fracture. The battle is won. Sixty thousand men die. Honda Shigetada is victorious.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that when the battle was done, Shigetada stood on the field and looked east, toward the capital, toward the Emperor who did not know that his son was alive, and did not speak. The chroniclers do not record what he thought. They record only the silence — the same silence that had accompanied him since the night of the raid, when he was two years old and alive and silent and cold.

===January 1601: The name===

The Emperor speaks the name. In January 1601, [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]] — the Crown Prince Yoshihito who had stood in the doorway of the nursery forty-three years earlier and did not speak — publicly recognizes Honda Shigetada as the lost Prince Fumihito. The secret that was a prison becomes a crown. The kidnapped prince goes home.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records the moment with a simplicity that carries the weight of the entire chronicle:

{{quote|The Emperor spoke the name. The name was Fumihito. The man who had been called Ichimatsu, who had been called Honda Shigetada, who had been called the prince by a man who meant to use him and by a man who meant to free him — the man heard the name, and the name was his, and it had always been his, and the mountain and the temple and the pine tree had kept it for him, and now the Emperor gave it back. The stolen prince went home. He had been gone for forty-three years. He had never left.|''Mitsukoji'', Book V}}

Shigetada was declared Crown Prince and ''Sei-i Taishōgun''. The [[Dual Sovereignty]] system was established — the political framework that would govern Okaiken for the next 250 years, in which the Emperor retained spiritual authority while the Shogun exercised military rule. The system that had its origins in a provincial lord's decision to adopt a temple foundling — a decision made because the lord valued competence over birth, and because the foundling, unknown to anyone, had been born to the throne.

===The crime and the punishment===

The ''Mitsukoji''{{'}}s framing of the Yuzawa Incident as the foundational crime of the Sengoku period is not merely a historiographical choice. It is a moral argument. The chronicle presents the incident as the moment at which the Ogasawara clan crossed a line that could not be uncrossed — the violation of the Imperial person, the deepest taboo in Sanese political culture — and set in motion the chain of events that would lead to their destruction.

The crime was the abduction of a child. The punishment was everything that followed: the Gonroku execution, the death of Motonobu, the Honto-ji betrayal, the Takigawa Terror, the Battle of Tone. The Ogasawara raised the man who would tear down everything they built, and they did not know it. They stole a prince and gave him to their enemies. They committed a crime and then forgot the evidence. They built an empire on the abduction of a child, and the child grew up and tore the empire down.

The ''Mitsukoji'' draws the throughline with a clarity that has shaped the Sanese understanding of the Sengoku period for over three centuries:

{{quote|The Yuzawa Incident was the first page of an anthology of revenge. The Ogasawara wrote it in the blood of a prince's uncle and the tears of a prince's mother. The Honda finished it in the blood of sixty thousand men at Tone and the silence of an emperor who spoke a name that had been kept for forty-three years. The anthology had many authors. The Ogasawara were the first. The Ogasawara were the last. The prince was the book.|''Mitsukoji'', Book V}}

The stolen prince went home. The anthology was closed. The original sin was redeemed — not by forgiveness, but by the forty-three-year arc of a life that began in violence and ended on a throne. The child who had been alive, and silent, and cold, in the arms of a dead nursemaid on the floor of a burning palace, became the Emperor who ruled the archipelago in peace. The Yuzawa Incident — the crime that began the Sengoku period — was the crime that ended it.

